---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFileBase64 from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeFileBase64.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="base64-to-file">
  <DevEncodeFileBase64 client:load mode="decode-file" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Turning a string back into a file</h2>
    <p>
      Paste the value, check the detected type, name the file and download it. The decoder accepts
      raw Base64, a full <code>data:</code> URI, the URL-safe alphabet, and input with line breaks or
      spaces in it. All of these occur when a value has been through a log, a YAML file or a terminal.
    </p>

    <h2>Identifying what you have</h2>
    <p>
      The file type comes from the decoded bytes, not from a label on the string. Some signatures to
      recognise:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Decoded bytes begin</th><th>File</th><th>Note</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>%PDF</code></td><td>PDF</td><td>Base64 starts <code>JVBERi0</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>PK</code> 03 04</td><td>ZIP</td><td>Also DOCX, XLSX, PPTX, JAR, APK, EPUB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1F 8B</td><td>gzip</td><td>Often a .tar.gz</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>-----BEGIN</code></td><td>PEM</td><td>Already text: Base64 inside a wrapper</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30 82</td><td>DER</td><td>Binary ASN.1: a certificate or key</td></tr>
          <tr><td>00 01 00 00 / <code>wOFF</code> / <code>wOF2</code></td><td>Font</td><td>TTF, WOFF, WOFF2</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>PEM files are a special case</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>.pem</code> certificate or key is already Base64, wrapped in
      <code>-----BEGIN…-----</code> lines. To decode one here, remove the header and footer lines and
      paste only the body. The result is DER (binary ASN.1), which is what
      <code>openssl x509 -inform der</code> expects. If you want the PEM itself, keep the header; it is
      already a text file.
    </p>

    <h2>When the download will not open</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The string was cut short. Base64 length must divide by four after padding. The usual cause is
        a partial copy from a terminal that wrapped or a database viewer that truncated the column.
      </li>
      <li>
        It is encoded twice. If the decoded bytes are printable Base64, decode them again.
      </li>
      <li>
        It was JSON-escaped. Sequences such as <code>\/</code> or <code>\n</code> in the value show
        that it was serialised into a JSON string. Unescape it first.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wrong alphabet. Values from JWTs use <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>. This tool handles them
        automatically, but other tools may not.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A note on decoding things you were sent</h2>
    <p>
      A decoded attachment is a real file that can do real things. Treat a Base64 blob from an email, a
      support ticket or a webhook like an unexpected download: check the detected type here first, and
      open it in a place where a mistake does no harm. Decoding is safe; opening may not be.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything happens in this tab</h2>
    <p>
      The bytes are rebuilt with <code>atob</code>, put into a <code>Blob</code> and passed to the
      download manager of the browser. No request leaves the page, so you can decode a value copied
      from production.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
